# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 2.7091e+04 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 3.9750 80.188 8.6890e+05 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~80 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 12.5430 38.721 4.1928e+08 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 1 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 F flare 12.5780 38.558 1.1428e+09 stylized  Light rises x9.0 in 0.14 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 38.7 km (95% of m0); split gen 0 at 38.4 km (95% of m0). Dust groups supply 70% of the rise and 62% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
5 B first_breakup 12.5999 38.456 1.8963e+09 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x4.5 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
6 - drag_overtakes_ablation 12.6569 38.193 3.6463e+09 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
7 F flare 13.2955 35.378 3.1323e+09 stylized  Light rises x2.0 in 0.12 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 1 at 35.4 km (33% of m0); split gen 1 at 35.2 km (32% of m0). Dust groups supply 30% of the rise and 30% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
8 P peak 13.3227 35.242 4.6673e+09 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+0.5 km from the peak of energy deposition (34.8 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 13.0 km/s).
9 D rolloff 13.8717 33.102 1.7153e+09 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.55 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 76% of v0, 63% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
10 F flare 13.9605 32.726 2.0001e+09 stylized  Light rises x1.4 in 0.10 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 1 at 32.6 km (14% of m0); split gen 1 at 32.4 km (14% of m0). Dust groups supply 33% of the rise and 22% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
11 b bump 14.7230 30.195 5.8120e+08 stylized  Small fluctuation, 14% above the local trend; coincides with a yield (gen 1) at 29.7 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
12 T tail 17.0096 24.126 4.6633e+06 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
